                <article class="seecom-news-item">
          <h2>SEECOM 2015 conference in Sofia: EU Delegation Chief in Bulgaria re-elected SEECOM Chairman</h2>

          <p class="seecom-news-date">
            18–20 September 2015 · Sofia, Bulgaria
          </p>

          <div class="seecom-justified">
            <p>
              More than 60 experts in government communications and public
              diplomacy gathered in Sofia, Bulgaria, on 18–20 September 2015
              for the fourth South East Europe Government Communication
              Conference, SEECOM 2015.
            </p>

            <p>
              The conference was organised by the Konrad-Adenauer-Stiftung
              Media Programme South East Europe and SEECOM, the professional
              network of senior public-sector communicators from 13 countries
              in South East Europe.
            </p>

            <p>
              In a written address to participants, Bulgarian President Rosen
              Plevneliev expressed his support for SEECOM’s mission and its
              efforts to promote professional standards and exchange among
              public-sector communicators in South East Europe. He noted that
              dialogue between public authorities and citizens, in which
              public communicators play a pivotal role, is essential for
              democratic development.
            </p>

            <p>
              At the General Assembly, held alongside the conference, members
              re-elected Ognian Zlatev, Head of the European Commission
              Representation in Bulgaria, as SEECOM Chairman for another term.
            </p>

            <p>
              The General Assembly also elected a new Steering Committee
              comprising Ivana Đurić, Assistant Director of the Serbian
              Government’s European Integration Office; Nebojša Regoje, Head
              of the Public Relations Office of the Ministry of Foreign
              Affairs of Bosnia and Herzegovina; Christian Spahr, Director of
              the KAS Media Programme South East Europe; and SEECOM Secretary
              General Vuk Vujnović, as an ex officio member.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <hr class="seecom-news-divider" />
                <article class="seecom-news-item">
          <h2>Government of Montenegro adopts project initiated at SEECOM 2014 conference</h2>

          <p class="seecom-news-date">
            9 November 2014 · Podgorica, Montenegro
          </p>

          <div class="seecom-justified">
            <p>
              The Government of Montenegro officially endorsed the project
              entitled <em>Public Administration Tailored to the Needs of
              Citizens and Businesses</em>, introducing an online platform
              where citizens, businesses, civil society organisations and
              public authorities could discuss ways to reduce red tape in
              local and central administration.
            </p>

            <p>
              The project was initiated at the SEECOM 2014 conference in
              Budva, Montenegro, on 27 September 2014, where the UK
              Government’s experience of developing and managing the
              <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/news/red-tape-challenge">
                Red Tape Challenge
              </a>
              initiative was presented.
            </p>

            <p>
              “This will be a way for civil society and the public sector to
              work together to formulate public policies, and the whole
              project will be managed by members of civil society,” Bojana
              Bošković, Director General for Business Environment at
              Montenegro’s Ministry of Finance, told the press after the
              Cabinet meeting on Thursday.
            </p>

            <p>
              By facilitating the transfer of the UK’s Red Tape Challenge
              experience and helping to introduce a similar initiative in
              Montenegro, the SEECOM 2014 conference demonstrated how
              international exchange of effective public communication
              practices can create new social and economic value in South
              East Europe.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <hr class="seecom-news-divider" />
                <article class="seecom-news-item">
          <h2>3rd SEECOM conference in Montenegro highlights the role of communication in social dialogue and citizen engagement</h2>

          <p class="seecom-news-date">
            26–28 September 2014 · Budva, Montenegro
          </p>

          <div class="seecom-justified">
            <p>
              Good public-sector communication that fosters meaningful
              dialogue between public authorities and civil society and
              enables citizens to actively participate in policymaking is
              invaluable for the development and progress of the countries
              of South East Europe. This was the key message of the 3rd
              South East Europe Public Sector Communication Conference,
              SEECOM 2014, which concluded in Budva on 28 September 2014.
            </p>

            <p>
              Entitled <em>Communication of Deeds</em>, the conference
              brought together senior public-sector communicators from ten
              countries in the region, as well as prominent communication
              experts and practitioners from Europe and the United States.
              It was supported by the Konrad-Adenauer-Stiftung Media
              Programme South East Europe, the Regional Cooperation
              Council, UNDP Montenegro, the British Embassy in Podgorica
              and the City of Budva.
            </p>

            <p>
              To promote its mission and activities in South East Europe,
              SEECOM adopted two key programmes. The first,
              <em>Transparency, Accountability and Public Participation</em>,
              aimed to provide public-sector communicators in the region
              with the knowledge and skills to promote government
              transparency, accountability and public participation in
              policymaking through effective communication practices.
            </p>

            <p>
              The second programme, <em>Communicating European Integration
              Affairs</em>, aimed to improve public-sector communicators’
              knowledge and skills in informing, educating and engaging
              citizens in European integration affairs.
            </p>

            <p>
              Ognian Zlatev, Head of the European Union Representation in
              Bulgaria, was elected SEECOM’s next Chairman. The conference
              also conferred honorary membership on keynote speaker Alex
              Aiken, Director of Communications for the UK Government, and
              Nadica Dujović, former SEECOM Steering Committee member and
              co-founder, in recognition of their exceptional contributions
              to the organisation’s mission and activities in South East
              Europe.
            </p>

            <p>
              The conference featured a presentation of the UK Government’s
              <em>Red Tape Challenge</em>, an online platform for broad
              public dialogue aimed at eliminating complicated
              administrative procedures and redundant legislation that
              burden businesses and citizens. The Fund for Active
              Citizenship (fAKT) also announced a small-grant opportunity
              for Montenegrin civil society organisations to develop a
              local communications platform aimed at reducing red tape in
              national and local administration.
            </p>

            <p>
              The conference aimed to promote public-sector communication
              as a catalyst for dialogue and collaboration between public
              authorities, civil society and citizens, with a view to
              improving public policies and creating new economic and
              social value. Participants used the opportunity to establish
              and renew regional contacts, showcase best practices and
              engage in discussions with leading communications experts
              on citizen engagement in public policymaking, communicating
              European integration affairs and building a common public
              diplomacy vision for South East Europe.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

